They’re examples of technical metaphors—terms that originated in computing but are vivid enough that engineers use them almost like everyday words.

Segfault (Segmentation Fault)

A segmentation fault (or segfault) happens when a program tries to access memory it isn’t allowed to.

Think of it like:

“You tried to open a filing cabinet in someone else’s locked office.”

The operating system immediately stops the program.

Pantheon analogy:

A semantic segfault would be trying to use a concept outside the context or assumptions that make it valid.

⸻

Bit Rot (Bitrot)

Originally, people imagined data literally “rotting.” Today it’s used more broadly.

It refers to software, code, or digital assets gradually becoming unusable because the environment changes around them—not because they were suddenly broken.

Examples:

* Old code no longer compiles.
* A website relies on libraries that disappear.
* A document references dead links.

Pantheon analogy:

Semantic bitrot occurs when meanings drift over time until old documents become harder to interpret.

⸻

Deadlock

A deadlock happens when two or more processes each wait on the other forever.

Classic example:

Person A:

“I’ll unlock the door after you.”

Person B:

“I’ll unlock mine after you.”

Neither moves.

Pantheon analogy:

Two ideas each require the other to be accepted before either can make progress.

⸻

Race Condition

A bug where the result depends on timing.

Example:
Two people edit the same document simultaneously.
Whichever saves last wins.

Pantheon analogy:

The order in which ideas arrive changes the final belief or interpretation.

⸻

Stack Overflow

A program keeps calling itself recursively until it runs out of call stack space.

Example:
A asks B.
B asks C.
C asks A.
Repeat until crash.

Pantheon analogy:

An explanation that endlessly depends on itself without grounding.

⸻

Memory Leak

A program allocates memory but never releases it.

Eventually performance slows and the system can crash.

Pantheon analogy:

Mental or organizational resources stay committed long after they’re useful.

⸻

Thrashing

A computer spends more time moving data around than doing useful work.

Pantheon analogy:

Constant context switching without meaningful progress.

⸻

Garbage Collection

Automatic cleanup of memory that is no longer referenced.

Pantheon analogy:

Periodically discarding obsolete assumptions, habits, or concepts so they don’t accumulate.

⸻

Technical names often become conceptual operators

Many of these terms escaped programming because they’re useful metaphors:

* Deadlock → negotiation, politics, psychology.
* Bottleneck → business and logistics.
* Feedback loop → biology, economics, AI.
* Bootstrap → entrepreneurship and self-improvement.
* Pipeline → manufacturing, software, machine learning.
* Daemon → background process in Unix, now often used metaphorically.

That’s one reason I liked Khiput. It has the same feel: a concrete, memorable word that names a specific failure mode. Once a community adopts it, people can say, “This design is khiput,” and everyone immediately understands they’re talking about a broken information structure rather than something generically “broken.”